      <p>Roofing is a high-value, high-consideration purchase. A replacement can be one of the biggest bills a homeowner pays, so people research, compare quotes and take their time. Yet a single storm can create a week of urgent demand. Google Ads has to handle both.</p>

      <h2>Split repairs, replacements and storm damage</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Repairs:</strong> leaks, missing shingles or tiles, flashing. Faster decisions, lower value, often urgent</li>
        <li><strong>Replacements:</strong> the highest-value work, with a long decision and several quotes. Worth their own campaign, ads and landing page</li>
        <li><strong>Storm damage:</strong> inspections and insurance work after hail or wind. Short, intense bursts of demand</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Different values and timelines mean different bids and budgets. Running them together lets cheap repair clicks soak up the budget that replacements need.</p>

      <h2>Be ready before the weather turns</h2>
      <p>Storm searches surge for a few days and then fall away. The contractors who win them have a campaign built in advance: ads about free inspections and help with insurance claims, a landing page that matches, and a budget that can be raised the same day.</p>
      <p>Outside storm periods, the same campaign can sit paused or on a small budget, ready to switch on.</p>

      <h2>Measure signed jobs, not form fills</h2>
      <p>The time between a roofing enquiry and a signed contract can be weeks. If the account only counts form fills, it will optimise for whoever fills in forms most cheaply, often people collecting quotes with no intention of buying soon.</p>
      <p>We record the source of every lead, then feed back which ones booked an inspection and which ones signed, with the job value. That shows the real cost per signed job by campaign, and lets bidding favour the searches that become contracts. The <a href="/tools/metrics-calculator/">metrics calculator</a> helps work out what you can afford per lead.</p>

      <h2>Price searches are worth reaching, carefully</h2>
      <p>Searches such as "roof replacement cost" come from homeowners early in the process. They are worth reaching, because whoever helps them understand the cost is often invited to quote. But they convert more slowly than "roofer near me", so they belong in their own ad group with their own bids and an offer like a free estimate.</p>

      <h2>Where roofing budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Roofing jobs, apprenticeships and training</li>
        <li>DIY and materials searches: how to patch a roof, shingles for sale</li>
        <li>Roof types you do not work on, or commercial work if you only do homes</li>
        <li>Areas outside your crews' range, especially during storms, when searches spread wide</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Check the search terms report weekly, and daily during storm bursts. <a href="/learn/how-to-find-negative-keywords/">How to find negative keywords</a> sets out the method.</p>

      <h2>Trust carries the click</h2>
      <p>For a purchase this large, homeowners look for proof before they call: reviews, years in business, warranties, licensing and financing options. Ads and landing pages should state the facts you can back up, and use sitelinks and callouts to show them. Specific, true claims beat generic ones every time.</p>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>If you already advertise, the first question is whether the account knows which leads became signed jobs. A <a href="/google-ads-audit/">$200 account analysis</a> answers that first. <a href="#enquiry">Ask for a free audit</a> and tell us the split between repairs and replacements you would like.</p>
